::SPEECH_U02_L04_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which list best shows required parts of a quality speech outline?
{
~A. Random facts, jokes, and unrelated notes.#This does not follow accepted speech format or quality expectations.
=B. Introduction, focus, main points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. These parts create a clear speech product format.
~C. A title only.#A title alone is incomplete for speech planning.
~D. A paragraph with no labels.#This makes the structure hard to follow.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which note best supports oral presentation?
{
~A. Talk about everything and stuff.#This is vague and does not help the speaker present clearly.
~B. Say all words from full script.#This may lead to reading instead of presenting.
~C. Random idea, maybe transition.#This is unclear and incomplete.
=D. Point 1: planner shows deadlines; evidence: assignments listed by date.#Correct. This note gives a clear cue for organized speaking.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence uses standard English conventions?
{
=A. My speech explains why note cards help speakers.#Correct. The sentence uses capitalization, subject-verb agreement, and clear wording.
~B. my speech explain why note cards helps speakers.#This has capitalization and subject-verb agreement errors.
~C. My speech explain why note-card cues helps speakers.#This has subject-verb agreement errors.
~D. my speech explains why note cards support delivery.#This needs capitalization at the beginning before it follows standard English conventions.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
A speech outline has an introduction, three points, and support, but no conclusion. What is the best revision?
{
~A. Remove the introduction.#Removing another required part weakens the format.
=B. Add a conclusion that restates the focus and closes the speech.#Correct. A conclusion is a required part that helps complete the speech format.
~C. Add unrelated details to make it longer.#Length does not fix a missing format part.
~D. Delete the support under each point.#Support is needed for a quality speech product.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L04_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which question best checks speech product quality?
{
~A. Is it as long as possible?#Length does not prove quality.
~B. Does it include random details?#Random details weaken quality.
=C. Does each part help the speaker communicate clearly?#Correct. Quality depends on whether the format supports clear communication.
~D. Does it avoid all structure?#Avoiding structure works against format-specific quality.
}
